    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Knee osteoarthritis grades describe what a radiologist can see on an X-ray, not how bad your knee feels or how it will end up.</strong> The scale used almost everywhere is Kellgren-Lawrence, which runs from grade 0 to grade 4, and grade 2 means definite bone spurs with joint space that may or may not be narrowed.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grade 2 is the mild end:</strong> definite osteophytes, possible joint space narrowing. Grades 1 and 2 are both routinely described as mild.</li>
        <li><strong>The grade tracks pain poorly:</strong> in one cohort of 658 adults over 40, radiographic knee osteoarthritis turned up in 20% of people who reported no pain at all.</li>
        <li><strong>It is not a one-way escalator:</strong> in an eight-year follow-up of knees graded 1 to 3, 85.7% stayed on a stable trajectory, losing about 0.5mm of joint space in total.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no collagen trial has recruited grade 4 knees, so nothing we sell has evidence at the severe end of this scale.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

