      <h2 id="grades">What do the knee osteoarthritis grades mean?</h2>
      <p>They are a description of two things on a weight-bearing X-ray: osteophytes, the small bony outgrowths at the joint margin, and joint space narrowing, which is the gap between the thigh bone and the shin bone on the film. The scale is called Kellgren-Lawrence, it has five steps, and nearly every Indian radiology report uses it even when it does not name it.</p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Usual label</th><th scope="col">What the radiologist is seeing</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">0</th><td>Normal</td><td>No radiographic features of osteoarthritis.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Doubtful</td><td>Doubtful joint space narrowing, possible osteophytic lipping.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Minimal / mild</td><td>Definite osteophytes, possible joint space narrowing.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Moderate</td><td>Multiple osteophytes, definite narrowing, sclerosis, possible bony deformity.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Severe</td><td>Large osteophytes, marked narrowing, severe sclerosis, definite deformity of the bone contour.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Two things are worth knowing about that table. The first is that grade 2 is defined by the <em>osteophyte</em> being definite, not by the narrowing — which is why some explainers say grade 2 has early narrowing and others say the joint space still looks close to normal. Both are describing knees that a radiologist would call grade 2.</p>
      <p>The second is that the reading depends on the reader. Assigning a Kellgren-Lawrence grade is a judgement made by the clinician looking at the film, and the same knee can come back a grade apart from two radiologists. That is one reason a single number deserves less weight than people give it.</p>

      <h2 id="pain-vs-grade">Does a higher grade mean more pain?</h2>
      <p>Only loosely, and the mismatch runs in both directions. Plenty of people with visible osteoarthritis have no pain, and plenty of people with a mild grade are in a great deal of it.</p>

      <div class="jr-stats">
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">20%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of adults over 40 who reported no knee pain still had radiographic knee osteoarthritis</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Ho-Pham+prevalence+radiographic+osteoarthritis+knee+self-reported+pain" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">PLoS ONE, 2014</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">23–31%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of people with grade 4 knees in two Korean cohorts reported no knee pain</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=absence+of+pain+advanced+radiographic+knee+osteoarthritis" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">BMC Musculoskeletal Disorders, 2020</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">25%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of asymptomatic, uninjured adults had osteophytes on knee MRI</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=prevalence+knee+osteoarthritis+features+MRI+asymptomatic+uninjured+adults" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Systematic review and meta-analysis, 2018</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>The first figure comes from 658 randomly sampled adults aged 40 and over, where the overall point prevalence of radiographic knee osteoarthritis was 34.2%, higher in women than men at 35.3% against 31.2%. One in five of the people with nothing to complain about had it on film.</p>
      <p>At the other extreme, a 2020 analysis looked specifically at grade 4 knees across three cohorts. Pain was absent in 23.5% and 31.2% of the two Korean groups and in 5.9% of the American group — a wide spread, which the authors attribute partly to the three studies defining knee pain with different questionnaires. The honest reading is that a considerable number of people with the worst-looking X-rays are not in pain, and the exact proportion depends on how you ask.</p>
      <p>The MRI data makes the same point earlier in life. In asymptomatic, uninjured knees, pooled estimates were 25% for osteophytes and 18% for bone marrow lesions, ranging from 4–14% in adults under 40 to 19–43% in adults of 40 and above. Findings on imaging are, to a large extent, a feature of ageing knees.</p>
      <p>Where is the gap widest? In mild disease. The discordance between knee pain and radiographic findings has been described as most prominent at the mild grades — exactly the grade 1 and grade 2 reports that worry people most, because that is when the diagnosis is new.</p>

      <h2 id="progression">Does grade 2 osteoarthritis always get worse?</h2>
      <p>No. Most grade 2 knees are fairly stable over years, a meaningful minority narrow further, and the number you read depends almost entirely on how long the study ran.</p>
      <p>The most reassuring figure comes from the longest follow-up. In a cohort of knees spanning grades 1 to 3 followed for eight years, 85.7% fell into a stable trajectory with roughly 0.5mm of joint space lost over the whole period. Smaller groups showed early or late progression.</p>
      <p>Shorter studies report higher rates, which sounds contradictory until you look at the denominators. Over 30 months, joint space narrowing progressed in 32% of knees that started at grade 2. One study with a mean follow-up of about 88 months put grade 2 progression at 60.9%, but it included only 64 knees in total, so that figure carries little weight. Over one to two years in a cohort with mild radiographic disease, progression occurred in 12% of participants.</p>
      <p>There is a genuinely counterintuitive finding here. When researchers measured the <em>speed</em> of joint space narrowing by grade, it was fastest in grade 2 knees at 0.35mm per year, and the speed showed a fair negative correlation with the grade — the more advanced the X-ray, the slower the loss. Across 34 studies the pooled average annual narrowing was 0.13mm, with observational studies averaging 0.17mm and randomised trials 0.08mm.</p>
      <p>So grade 2 is not the stage to panic about, but it is not the stage to ignore either. It appears to be the stage at which structural change is moving fastest, which makes it the stage at which the things that genuinely work are most worth starting. Activity level matters here: in Osteoarthritis Initiative data, both higher and lower physical activity were associated with more narrowing over 48 months than moderate activity, specifically in men and in knees that started at grade 2.</p>
      <p>One caveat on all of it. Joint space narrowing on an X-ray is a proxy, not a measurement of cartilage. It can reflect cartilage loss, meniscal extrusion, or both.</p>

      <h2 id="do-you-need-xray">Do you even need an X-ray?</h2>
      <p>For a typical presentation, no. NICE guideline NG226 says to diagnose osteoarthritis clinically, without imaging, in people aged 45 or over who have activity-related joint pain and either no morning stiffness or stiffness lasting no more than 30 minutes. Imaging is not recommended routinely unless there are atypical features or something that suggests a different diagnosis. The stated reason is the one this article has been describing: there is often a poor link between what shows on an X-ray, MRI or ultrasound and the symptoms a person actually has.</p>
      <p>In India the X-ray usually happens anyway. Films are inexpensive and quick, radiology is often walk-in, and a report with a grade on it feels like a real answer. There is a cost to that, and it has now been measured.</p>
      <p>A randomised trial published in <em>PLOS Medicine</em> in 2025 presented 617 people with a hypothetical painful knee and a GP consultation delivered as a two-minute video, in one of three forms: a clinical explanation with no X-ray, a radiographic explanation, or a radiographic explanation with the images shown. People shown the images rated joint replacement surgery as more necessary than those given the clinical explanation — a mean of 4.5 against 3.3, a difference of 1.1 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.5 to 1.8. Coverage of the trial put it as a 36% higher perceived need for surgery. Beliefs about whether exercise and physical activity would help did not change at all.</p>
      <p>It was a hypothetical scenario answered online once, with no follow-up, so it measures stated beliefs rather than what people go on to do. But the direction is worth knowing before you ask for a film: seeing your own X-ray makes surgery feel more necessary without making exercise feel any less useful.</p>

