      <h2 id="what-to-do">What actually helps, at any grade</h2>
      <p>The first-line answers are the same from grade 1 to grade 4, and they are not supplements. The American College of Rheumatology's updated osteoarthritis guidance keeps weight management and exercise among its strongest recommendations, alongside topical anti-inflammatories where appropriate, and adds a conditional recommendation for GLP-1 receptor agonists in people with knee osteoarthritis and obesity who need help reaching a healthier weight. The same update strongly recommends <em>against</em> glucosamine, chondroitin and the two combined, for knee, hip and hand osteoarthritis alike.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Strengthen the muscle around the joint.</strong> Guidelines recommend exercise over no exercise regardless of grade. <a href="/pages/exercises-for-knee-pain">Exercises for knee pain</a> sets out which ones the trials rank highest.</li>
        <li><strong>Lose load if there is load to lose.</strong> Roughly four pounds of compressive force comes off the knee during daily activity for every pound of body weight lost. Reviews put the best outcomes at weight loss of about 7% of starting weight or more, and the results are better when diet is combined with exercise rather than either alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Treat the pain so you can do the first two.</strong> That is a conversation with your doctor, and topical options are often the starting point rather than tablets.</li>
        <li><strong>Protein and co-factors, as support.</strong> Muscle you are asking to adapt needs protein, and bone needs calcium, vitamin D and magnesium. If your reason for reading this was a bone density result rather than a knee X-ray, <a href="/pages/osteopenia-vs-osteoporosis">osteopenia vs osteoporosis</a> explains how that report is scored.</li>
        <li><strong>Reassess at 12 weeks, not at two.</strong> Nothing in this list works in a fortnight.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Surgery sits at the end of that sequence, not in the middle of it. Joint replacement is considered for severe symptoms after appropriate non-surgical treatment has been tried, and the decision is driven by how much the knee limits your life rather than by the number on the film. Some people with severe X-ray changes manage comfortably; some with moderate changes are significantly disabled.</p>

      <h2 id="supplements">Where a supplement fits, honestly</h2>
      <p>Behind everything in the list above, and only for part of the scale. This is the part of the article that works against us, so it is worth being precise.</p>
      <p>The collagen peptide trials in knee osteoarthritis recruited mild to moderate knees. A meta-analysis of collagen peptide randomised trials reported significant pain relief against placebo, and the included trials typically used Kellgren-Lawrence grade 1 to 3 as their entry criterion. Individual trials go narrower still: one 180-day trial of low-molecular-weight collagen peptides enrolled 80 adults aged 40 to 75 at grade 1 or 2 only. <strong>Grade 4 knees were not recruited.</strong> If your report says grade 4, the honest position is that the collagen evidence has not been tested in knees like yours, and no part of it should be read as applying to you.</p>
      <p>On the ingredient most people buy specifically for knees, our own formula does not clear the bar either. Skelecoll contains 300mg of glucosamine HCl, a fraction of the 1,500mg used in the major glucosamine trials, and those trials did not find it better than placebo anyway. It sits in the formula as a supporting co-factor at a co-factor dose, and the ACR now recommends against glucosamine outright. We would rather say that here than let a label imply otherwise. <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">Collagen for joint pain</a> goes through the trial evidence in full, and <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">how much collagen per day</a> covers the doses the trials actually used.</p>

