      <h2 id="doctor">When a knee needs a doctor, not a grade</h2>
      <p>Some knee problems are not osteoarthritis, and a grade on an old film can delay the diagnosis that matters. Get the knee seen the same day if it is hot, red and swollen with fever or chills, especially without an injury; if it swelled within minutes of an injury; if it looks deformed; or if you cannot put weight on it at all.</p>
      <p>See a doctor promptly, rather than waiting, if the knee locks and will not straighten, if it gives way, if there is numbness or tingling below the knee, or if pain regularly wakes you at night. Morning stiffness that lasts well beyond 30 to 60 minutes, especially in several joints on both sides, points away from osteoarthritis and towards an inflammatory arthritis that needs blood tests rather than a knee X-ray. And if the noise rather than the pain is what brought you here, <a href="/pages/knee-cracking-popping">knee cracking and popping</a> covers what it does and does not mean.</p>
      <p>One note on how common this is in India. A systematic review and meta-analysis of population studies put knee osteoarthritis prevalence at 20.24%, with individual studies ranging from 3.66% to 56%; a pan-India community study using the Kellgren-Lawrence scale found 28.7%; a rural Tamil Nadu study of 427 adults aged 40 and over found 34.6%; and a pooled estimate in elderly Indians reached 47%. A grade on a report puts you in very large company, which is the opposite of what it feels like at the time.</p>

