      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What does grade 2 osteoarthritis of the knee mean?</summary>
          <p>On the Kellgren-Lawrence scale, grade 2 means the radiologist can see definite osteophytes, which are small bony outgrowths at the joint margin, with joint space narrowing that may or may not be present. It is usually labelled minimal or mild. The grade describes the X-ray only, and says very little about how much pain or stiffness you will have.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is grade 2 knee osteoarthritis serious?</summary>
          <p>Grades 1 and 2 are both routinely described as mild, and most grade 2 knees do not deteriorate quickly. In an eight-year follow-up of knees graded 1 to 3, 85.7% stayed on a stable trajectory with about 0.5mm of joint space lost in total. Grade 2 is, however, the stage at which narrowing appears to move fastest, at around 0.35mm per year, so it is a sensible point to start exercise and weight management rather than to wait.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can knee osteoarthritis grades go backwards?</summary>
          <p>No. Osteophytes and lost joint space do not reverse, and no supplement, injection or exercise programme has been shown to move a knee from grade 3 back to grade 2 on an X-ray. What can improve is pain, stiffness and function, which is what treatment actually targets. Be wary of any product that implies it rebuilds or regrows cartilage.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does grade 4 osteoarthritis always need a knee replacement?</summary>
          <p>No. Joint replacement is considered for severe symptoms after non-surgical treatment has been tried properly, and the decision rests on how much the knee limits your life rather than on the grade. A 2020 analysis of grade 4 knees found that 23.5% and 31.2% of people in two Korean cohorts reported no knee pain at all, alongside 5.9% in an American cohort. Your surgeon decides this with you, not from the film alone.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need an X-ray to diagnose knee osteoarthritis?</summary>
          <p>Usually not. NICE guideline NG226 says osteoarthritis can be diagnosed clinically without imaging in people aged 45 or over with activity-related joint pain and morning stiffness lasting no more than 30 minutes, and advises against routine imaging unless there are atypical features. The reason is that X-ray, MRI and ultrasound findings correlate poorly with symptoms. An X-ray is still useful when the picture is unusual or another diagnosis is possible.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen work for grade 3 or grade 4 knee osteoarthritis?</summary>
          <p>There is no evidence at grade 4, because the trials did not recruit those knees. Collagen peptide trials in knee osteoarthritis have typically used Kellgren-Lawrence grade 1 to 3 as their entry criterion, and some used grade 1 to 2 only, so the pooled pain benefit reported against placebo applies to mild and moderate knees. Anyone selling you collagen for a grade 4 knee is going beyond the evidence.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does my knee hurt more than my X-ray grade suggests?</summary>
          <p>Because pain and radiographs come apart routinely, and the gap is widest in mild disease. Pain in osteoarthritis involves the joint lining, the surrounding muscle, sleep and sensitisation, none of which appear on a film. In one cohort of 658 adults over 40, radiographic knee osteoarthritis was present in 20% of people who reported no pain, and the reverse happens just as often. A low grade does not mean your pain is not real or not worth treating.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I ask to see my own knee X-ray?</summary>
          <p>There is a reason to be cautious. A randomised trial in PLOS Medicine in 2025 presented 617 people with a hypothetical knee consultation and found that those shown X-ray images rated joint replacement as more necessary than those given a clinical explanation, by 4.5 against 3.3 on its rating scale, with no change in how helpful they thought exercise would be. It was a hypothetical online scenario rather than real practice, but the finding suggests that looking at the image can shift you towards surgery without shifting you towards the things that help most.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

