        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why does my knee hurt more than my X-ray grade suggests?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Because pain and radiographs come apart routinely, and the gap is widest in mild disease. Pain in osteoarthritis involves the joint lining, the surrounding muscle, sleep and sensitisation, none of which appear on a film. In one cohort of 658 adults over 40, radiographic knee osteoarthritis was present in 20% of people who reported no pain, and the reverse happens just as often. A low grade does not mean your pain is not real or not worth treating." } },
